{
  "image_name": "49f3ce87b1938fc5c3e0d14ecf2c567b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a challenge–response protocol for verifying a prover, and how it can be broken by an adversary that inserts an extractor. Overall, it contrasts normal operation with an attack.\n\nOn the left, a randomness source generates a challenge and some prover-side output, which are checked in a verification step. If verification passes, an extractor is used to recover the final output; if it fails, the protocol aborts. This panel conveys the intended logic: random challenge generation, prover response, verification, and conditional extraction.\n\nThe middle part shows the honest interaction between two parties, A and B, coordinated through a coin flip that produces a shared challenge sent to a verifier or device labeled Q. A and B each provide random inputs, receive responses, and in this honest case B accepts.\n\nThe right part shows an adversarial version. A shaded “Adversary” box intercepts A’s side and appears to manipulate both A’s local randomness and the response from Q, producing altered values marked with primes. These modified messages are forwarded to B, causing an inconsistent final response. In contrast to the honest case, B now rejects. The key visual message is that the extractor-based attack lets the adversary bias or forge outputs while evading the original protocol structure.",
